1... d5 2. exd5 Nf6 { Black is trying to be clever by avoiding all the main lines with Qxd5.
Nf6 here is the second best move for black behind Qxd5 and according to engines it is 3 centipawn worse then Qxd5 (that practically nothing, to keep things into perspective that's 0.03 pawns).
The practical problem with Nf6 for white is that is radically transforms the position for a typical Qxd5 Scandinavian position into something else completely different, in fact black is planning Nxd5 instead of Qxd5 no matter what white does here.
White has 2 top moves here: Nf3 or d4. Both d4 and Nf3 invite black to transpose back to familiar territory/the main lines with Nf3 Qxd5. It turns out that black's best pet is to transpose back but in practice most humans take back with the f6 knight in this position after d4 or Nf3.
Nc3 is the 9th best move here at s barely 0.00, this is quite shocking if compare it to the main line (Qxd5 Nc3). We can turn this into a rule :
RULE : In the Scandinavian, rushing Nc3 is almost always bad unless it comes with tempo on the black queen.
Most players online go Nc3 by pure habit/inertia assuming the c3 square is the right square for the c3 knight and that we need to rush that move but this is completely false and reveals 0 understanding of the reasons behind the moves.
Note that d4 or Nf3 are not automatic moves because they require a bit of calculation: At the very least what happens after d4/Nf3 Bg4? } 3. d4 { Note that we can still transpose back into the main line say after Qxd5 Nc3 Qa5 but from this move order black almost nver does that and instead goes Nxd5 to get an independent position from all the main line Scandinavian theory. } 3... Qxd5 4. Nc3 Qa5 { This an extremely rare yet possible way to transpose back into a mainline position from a different move order, (here with Nf6 first instead of the usual Qxd5 Nc3 first). } *


